  <section class="osc-section">
    <h2>Understanding Ohio Withholding: State, City &amp; School District Taxes</h2>
    <p>Calculating take-home pay in the State of Ohio is uniquely complex due to three distinct layers of state and local taxation that apply directly to earned wage income:</p>

    <div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(240px,1fr));gap:1rem;margin:1.25rem 0;">
      <div style="background:#f8fafc;padding:1.15rem;border-radius:8px;border-left:4px solid #2563eb;">
        <strong style="color:#0f172a;display:block;margin-bottom:0.35rem;">1. Ohio State Income Tax</strong>
        <span style="font-size:0.88rem;color:#475569;line-height:1.55;display:block;">Ohio features a generous 0% tax floor on income up to $26,050. Income between $26,050 and $100,000 is taxed at a flat 2.75%, while earnings above $100,000 are subject to a top marginal rate of 3.125% (under current Ohio Department of Taxation brackets).</span>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#f8fafc;padding:1.15rem;border-radius:8px;border-left:4px solid #10b981;">
        <strong style="color:#0f172a;display:block;margin-bottom:0.35rem;">2. Municipal Income Taxes (RITA/CCA)</strong>
        <span style="font-size:0.88rem;color:#475569;line-height:1.55;display:block;">Over 600 Ohio cities and villages levy local municipal income tax ranging from 1.0% to 3.0%. Employers withhold based on where you physically perform work, while your residence municipality may grant a credit for taxes paid to your workplace municipality.</span>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#f8fafc;padding:1.15rem;border-radius:8px;border-left:4px solid #f59e0b;">
        <strong style="color:#0f172a;display:block;margin-bottom:0.35rem;">3. School District Income Tax (SDIT)</strong>
        <span style="font-size:0.88rem;color:#475569;line-height:1.55;display:block;">Roughly 200 Ohio public school districts levy an additional local income tax of 0.5% to 2.0%. Unlike municipal taxes, SDIT is based strictly on your home residence address (Form IT-1040 4-digit district code).</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3>Major Ohio City Tax Rates (2026 Reference)</h3>
    <table class="osc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>City</th><th>Municipal Rate</th><th>Administering Agency</th><th>Resident Credit</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Columbus</strong></td><td>2.50%</td><td>City of Columbus Revenue Division</td><td>100% credit up to 2.5%</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Cleveland</strong></td><td>2.50%</td><td>Central Collection Agency (CCA)</td><td>100% credit up to 2.5%</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Cincinnati</strong></td><td>1.80%</td><td>City of Cincinnati Tax Division</td><td>100% credit up to 1.8%</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Toledo</strong></td><td>2.50%</td><td>City of Toledo Tax Division</td><td>100% credit up to 2.5%</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Akron</strong></td><td>2.50%</td><td>City of Akron Tax Division</td><td>100% credit up to 2.25%</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Dayton</strong></td><td>2.50%</td><td>City of Dayton Division of Revenue</td><td>100% credit up to 2.5%</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3>Maximizing Take-Home Pay Through Pre-Tax Deductions</h3>
    <p>One of the most effective strategies to lower your total Ohio tax obligation is contributing to qualified pre-tax benefit programs. Contributions to an employer-sponsored <strong>Traditional 401(k) or 403(b)</strong> directly reduce your federal and Ohio state taxable wage base. Enrolling in a <strong>Health Savings Account (HSA)</strong> or <strong>Flexible Spending Account (FSA)</strong>, along with pre-tax employer health insurance premiums under Section 125 cafeteria plans, also exempts those wages from FICA taxes (6.2% Social Security and 1.45% Medicare), generating immediate 20% to 35% net savings.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="osc-section">
    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <% const _faqs = [["How do I convert my annual salary to biweekly pay in Ohio?", "Divide your annual salary by 26 to get gross biweekly pay. Then subtract federal income tax, Ohio state tax, local municipal tax, Social Security (6.2%), and Medicare (1.45%) from that gross to get your estimated biweekly take-home pay."], ["What is take-home pay on a \\$60,000 salary in Ohio?", "A single filer earning \\$60,000 in Columbus takes home approximately \\$43,500–\\$45,000 per year, or roughly \\$1,670–\\$1,730 biweekly, after all taxes. Married filers and those with pre-tax deductions will take home more."], ["How does a raise affect my Ohio take-home pay?", "A raise increases gross pay but may push some income into a higher marginal bracket. Use this calculator to compare your current and new salary to see the exact after-tax difference. In most cases, you always take home more after a raise — a higher bracket only applies to the income above the bracket threshold."], ["Does Ohio have a standard deduction for state income tax?", "Ohio uses a threshold approach rather than a standard deduction. Income up to \\$26,050 is taxed at 0%, effectively creating a zero-tax floor. Amounts above that are taxed at progressive rates."], ["What happens if I live in one Ohio city and work in another?", "Under Ohio Revised Code Chapter 718, your employer must withhold municipal tax for the city where you work. When you file your annual local tax return, your resident city will typically grant a reciprocal credit for the taxes paid to the workplace city up to your home city rate. If your home city rate is higher than your work city rate, you owe the difference."], ["Are remote and work-from-home employees taxed at home or office location in Ohio?", "Under current Ohio law, remote employees are taxed based on their actual physical work location. If you work from home in Dublin (2.0%) for an employer headquartered in Columbus (2.5%), your employer should withhold Dublin local tax for the days you work from your home."]]; _faqs.forEach(function(f) { %>
